<BODY bgcolor="#ffffff">
<BR><BR>All of the documents I could find about the Space Shuttle, more information <A HREF="Shuttle/AAA_Lots_Of_Documents_Readme.txt">is here</A>. My main page about all the other things I do is <a href="index.html">Here</a>. If you DO want to point to NASA Space Shuttle information or a web page I STRONGLY suggest you download it when you find it. While updating my main page I clicked on the links from many years ago and those sites are gone. An example is the reference to the <A HREF="http://science.ksc.nasa.gov/shuttle/technology/sts-newsref/et.html#et" target="_blank">External Tank</A>, now only to be found on the Wayback Machine on Archive.Org, i.e. <A HREF="https://web.archive.org/web/20220401054815/http://science.ksc.nasa.gov/shuttle/technology/sts-newsref/et.html#et" target="_blank">External Tank</A> (minus any graphics on the page). NASA is slowly closing all the web sites that they had documenting the Space Shuttle.<BR><BR>
Note:<BR>
For files larger than 100 MB Google Drive will state that the file has not been scanned for viruses and ask you if you want to "Download anyway". Since none of the below files are executable you can click on the download button.
<BR><BR>

<BR>Here is the <A HREF="https://1drv.ms/u/s!AsoLDJx_szsuc_zSFFU0B1gKkFc?e=tyej6E"> Bing Drive share Link</A> so that you can download all the files easily
<BR> --- Or ---
<BR>Here is the <A HREF="https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1-Dlxg1P6-W3FfqxORm7a7hfmTIEf2lWy?usp=sharing"> Google Drive share Link</A> so that you can download all the files easily.
<BR> --- Or ---
<BR>Just browse the directory <A HREF="Shuttle/">Directory of shuttle files</A> so that you can look at all the files in a folder structure. Your choice.
<BR><BR>
Ever wonder what it took to build Pad-B for the space Shuttle? <A HREF="https://www.16streets.com/MacLaren/MacLaren'sStoriesContents.html" target="_blank">James MacLaren</A> tells you all about the people and the steel, pipes and wrires behind the <A HREF="https://www.16streets.com/39-B/39-B%20Construction%20History%20-%20Space%20Shuttle%20-%20Page%20001.html" target="_blank">construction of Pad-B</A> for launching the Space Shuttle.
<BR><BR>
In addition here is a behind the scenes look at <A HREF="https://spaceflight.training/" target="_blank">Spaceflight training</A> and <A HREF="https://spaceflight.training/library.htm" target="_blank">additional documents about the shuttle</A>.
<BR><BR>
Well done PBS station WLRN Documentary covering the Space Shuttle Program, <A HREF="https://www.pbs.org/video/when-we-were-shuttle-zdzfps/" target="_blank">When We Were Shuttle</A>, here is the <A HREF="https://whenwewereshuttle.org/" target="_blank">website</A> with more information. I worked with Travis Thompson quite a bit, great guy. I also worked with Bill Carr as he was coming on shift and I was going off third shift.
<BR><BR>
David Rumsey has Space Shuttle <A HREF="https://www.davidrumsey.com/luna/servlet/view/all/who/Aerospace+Center/what/Space+shuttles" target="_blank">Mission Charts</A> showing what the orbits of various missions were at different launch inclinations from 1982 to 1985. These were hard to get at KSC when I was there, I got a couple like the <A HREF="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1WsaQBezHMY4TAon75i5X6ZmCpPMYvg7a/view?usp=sharing" target="_blank">Mission Chart for STS-66</A> (I scanned this one in, not nearly as good as the ones from David Rumsey).
<BR><BR>
Jonathan H Ward has written multiple books on the Space Program. The <A HREF="https://apollolaunchcontrol.com/" target="_blank">Apollo Launch Control</A> website has a TON of information about technology and processes used at Kennedy Space Center during Apollo.
<BR><BR>
